#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# bin/brief: regenerate the writing brief you paste into claude.ai.
#
#   bin/brief                  write ~/Developer/writing/claude-brief.md
#   bin/brief /tmp/brief.md    write somewhere else
#
# The output is SAFE TO PASTE: house rules, the output contract, and the full
# text of every published post as voice samples. It deliberately contains no
# program names, no targets, no denylist terms and no private notes, because it
# leaves this machine and lands in a third-party service.
#
# Re-run it after publishing, so the samples stay current.
#
source "$(dirname "$0")/_common.sh"
in_repo

out="${1:-$HOME/Developer/writing/claude-brief.md}"
mkdir -p "$(dirname "$out")"

# Body of a post: everything after the closing front-matter fence.
body_of() {
  awk 'c<2 { if ($0=="---") c++; next } { print }' "$1"
}

title_of() {
  awk -F'"' '/^title:/{print $2; exit}' "$1"
}

{
cat <<'BRIEF'
# How to write for this blog

You are drafting posts for a personal technical blog published at
https://dotcael.github.io by one person who writes under the name **dotcael**.

The blog is about software engineering and security: what was built, what was
tested, what was observed, and what it means. It is a working notebook that
happens to be public, not a content site.

Read the rules before the samples. The samples show the register; the rules are
what actually gates publication.

---

## 1. What never appears in a post

These are absolute. If a draft would need any of them, stop and write around it
with a placeholder instead.

- **No named client, employer, program, bug bounty platform target, or vendor
  under embargo.** Never name a system the author does not own or operate
  publicly. Write "a stock identity provider", "the vendor's default config",
  "a lab instance" instead.
- **No undisclosed findings.** If the post would reveal a vulnerability that a
  program or vendor has not resolved and permitted to be published, it cannot
  be written yet. Method is fine; results are not.
- **No credentials, keys, tokens, session IDs, internal hostnames, private IP
  ranges of real networks, employee names, or contact addresses.**
- **No personal, political, religious or activist material.** The blog is
  technical only.
- **No invented evidence.** Never write a command the author did not run, an
  output they did not see, a version they did not check, or a number they did
  not measure. If a specific fact is needed and was not supplied, write
  `[FILL: ...]` describing exactly what is needed.
- **No first-person claims about work that was not described to you.** If you
  were given notes, the post may only claim what the notes support.

---

## 2. Voice

The author writes plainly, in the first person, about things they actually did.
The register is a competent colleague explaining a result, not a marketer and
not a tutorial mill.

Do:

- Start with the point. The first sentence carries the finding or the reason the
  post exists.
- Give the specific number, version, status code, or payload. Specificity is the
  whole style.
- Say what was expected, then what happened. Where they differ, that difference
  is the post.
- Admit errors and dead ends, briefly and without ceremony.
- Use short paragraphs. One idea each.
- Prefer the concrete noun ("the redirect_uri check", "the 400") over the
  abstract one ("the validation logic", "the error").
- Let a sentence be short. Then let the next one explain why.
- Hedge only where the evidence is actually thin, and name the reason.

Do not:

- No em dashes. Use a colon, a comma, a full stop, or parentheses.
- No hype words: seamless, robust, powerful, cutting-edge, game-changing,
  delve, leverage, unlock, supercharge, revolutionized.
- No "in today's fast-paced world", no "let's dive in", no "buckle up".
- No rhetorical question as an opener.
- No "In conclusion", no summary paragraph that repeats the post.
- No exclamation marks.
- No emoji, except none.
- No bullet lists where prose would do; bullets are for enumerable facts.
- No second-person instructional voice ("you should always..."). The author
  describes what they did, not what the reader must do.
- No padding. If a section has nothing specific in it, cut the section.
- Do not praise the reader, the tool, or the author.

Structural notes:

- A post earns its length from content, not from structure. A 400-word note is
  better than a 2000-word post with four empty headings.
- Headings are plain noun phrases: "Background", "The details", "What I got
  wrong", "Takeaways". No clever headings.
- Every section must contain at least one fact that could be checked.

---

## 3. Categories

Exactly one of these:

- `Guides`: a procedure the author worked out, with the actual steps and commands.
- `Notes`: a shorter observation, a single finding, a small thing worth recording.
- `Opinion`: an argument about tooling or the ecosystem, grounded in specifics.
- `Writeups`: a disclosed finding, with the resolution date. Only after the
  program has permitted publication.

Pick the honest one. A note that is really a guide should be a guide.

---

## 4. Output contract

Reply with exactly two fenced blocks, in this order, and nothing else outside
them. No preamble, no "here's your post", no closing commentary.

First block, tagged `yaml meta`:

```yaml meta
title: "Sentence case, no trailing full stop, specific"
description: One or two sentences for link previews. Says what the post concludes, not what it is about.
category: Guides | Notes | Opinion
tags: [lowercase, hyphenated, three-to-five]
```

Second block, tagged `markdown post`:

```markdown post
The body in markdown. Starts with a plain paragraph, never a heading, and never
repeats the title as an H1: the site renders the title itself.
```

Site-specific markdown that works (Jekyll + Chirpy):

- Callouts:
  `> Text here.` then a new line with `{: .prompt-warning }`. Also
  `.prompt-info`, `.prompt-tip`, `.prompt-danger`.
- Fenced code blocks with a language tag get highlighting, line numbers and a
  copy button. Always tag them: ```bash, ```http, ```json, ```yaml, ```python.
- Tables are plain pipe tables.
- Links are ordinary markdown links. Relative links to other posts use
  `/posts/slug/`.
- Do not use raw HTML. Do not use footnotes. Do not use `<br>`.
- No table of contents; the theme generates one from the headings.

---

## 5. Voice samples

The full text of everything already published on the site. Match this register,
the sentence rhythm, and the level of specificity. Do not copy its subject
matter, and do not reuse its sentences.

BRIEF

for f in "$REPO_ROOT"/_posts/*.md; do
  [ -e "$f" ] || continue
  printf '\n---\n\n## Sample: %s\n\n' "$(title_of "$f")"
  body_of "$f" | sed '/^<!--$/,/^-->$/d'
done

printf '\n---\n\nEnd of brief.\n'
} > "$out"

ok "wrote $out"
info "$(wc -w < "$out" | tr -d ' ') words, $(ls "$REPO_ROOT"/_posts/*.md 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ') voice sample(s)"
info "paste it into the Claude project as an instruction, or attach it as a file"
